# Financial Allocation for Scheduled Castes and Backward Communities
Tamil title: நிதி ஒதுக்கீடு - தாழ்த்தப்பட்டோர்

Speech delivered in the Legislative Assembly on 14 February 1961.

Mr Deputy Speaker, as we examine the amount allotted in this year's budget and the manner in which it will be spent to bring backward and Scheduled Caste communities toward equality in society, I first wish to explain a remark of mine that has caused distress among several friends belonging to the Scheduled Castes.

Ministers repeatedly say that DMK members are unfit for political activity and possess no political knowledge. In answer, I asked why, when everyone else could become a minister, a DMK member should not. Some have converted this into the accusations that I look down upon Adi Dravidars, insult their leader Kakkan, and even disparage Tamil. They mistakenly imagine such charges will truly injure me. If they expect to win by fastening blame upon my head, they will soon be disappointed. I do not believe this debate can damage the DMK's long and affectionate relationship with Adi Dravidar people.

As I said in this House two years ago, we wholeheartedly welcome every effort to uplift Adi Dravidars and backward classes. We are glad that larger sums are allotted each year, and I have previously acknowledged Kakkan's earnest attention to this work.

When I asked why I could not become a minister, one part of my sentence was omitted. I said, "When Kamaraj and Kakkan have become ministers, why should I not?" I did not single out Kakkan. It was neither a reference to his caste nor a personal slight. I respect him.

There are Adi Dravidar people involved in our movement who say, "You are the people suited to us; you will protect us." If Kakkan comes with me one day, I would like to introduce him to them. I do not understand the laughter. Are members laughing because I said I would introduce them to Kakkan? They can find better reasons to laugh.

Some say that mentioning his Tamil-medium education insults Tamil. Yet his English replies in this House and his explanations of official reports show that he knows English. Nor am I so ignorant of education as to think that only one who studied in English is learned. Khrushchev may not know English; we do not therefore regard him as deficient.

Education in Tamil is still education. I do not imagine Kakkan or Kamaraj needed a university merely to acquire ability. My point was that they became ministers directly, rather than first acquiring administrative experience outside and then entering office. I said that if they could become ministers at the outset, so could I. It was not said to demean either their community or them personally. Abandoning that misunderstanding will first bring comfort to their own minds and prevent false ideas from being cultivated in this House.

I do not say this under compulsion to make peace. Kakkan himself knows that I do not speak of anyone in a degrading manner. When he visited Kanchipuram for campaigning, I invited him to my home and asked him at least to take tea before leaving. He declined. Of the eight ministers here, Kakkan is the only one I invited. From that fact he should understand my regard for him.

Whether one person's appointment as minister uplifts an entire community is itself open to debate. A Congress member said here last year, "We cannot regard our community as uplifted merely because Kakkan, who belongs to it, has become a minister." I esteem Kakkan not simply because he is a minister but because he is a good man, and indeed because he belongs to the Adi Dravidar community. Political debate should not be used to foster misinterpretation.

One member began by alleging that Adi Dravidars were refused admission to tea shops flying the black-and-red flag, and then amended this to say Adi Dravidars wearing khadi were refused. If the complaint concerns khadi wearers, I need not answer it. But if any shop flying our movement's flag denies entry because a person is Adi Dravidar, no one could bring greater disgrace upon our flag and organisation. If members come with me, I can show them both the hardships imposed upon people and the degrading practice of cutting a channel to pour water from a distance.

The fault does not lie in the flag; it lies in a cruel custom of long standing. We must ask whether our efforts have succeeded in destroying that custom. Statistical accounting alone will not solve it.

The report points to an allocation of roughly two crore and fifty-five lakh rupees and calls it a matter for pride. Whether or not it justifies pride, it is certainly welcome. But we must apply elementary economics. Work that could be done last year for two crore cannot necessarily be done this year for three crore.

The physical target, the actual amount of progress in the work undertaken, matters more than the nominal financial total. Inflation, expansion of the note supply, depreciation of money and rising prices mean that something completed for one lakh ten years ago may now require ten lakh. When claiming that expenditure rose from Rs 65 lakh in 1950-51 to Rs 355 lakh today, five times as much, the Government must also calculate how far the rupee's value has fallen and prices have risen before declaring its achievement.

Careful comparison also shows that the rise is not unique to this department. About six crore rupees is allotted to the police while roughly three crore goes to Scheduled Caste welfare. Expenditure has risen in every department. This sum has increased in the same general manner and does not by itself prove special attention. I ask the Minister to forget for a moment that I sit in Opposition and consider the argument.

If we proceed slowly and step by step, the spectre of untouchability that has survived for generations will remain for many generations more. We need an immediate, comprehensive, practicable and non-partisan plan grounded in economics, with a dedicated administrative mechanism and funding, to abolish untouchability decisively.

I expected the First Five-Year Plan to make proper provision for raising the standard of living in Adi Dravidar settlements, and then expected it of the Second Plan. Agricultural welfare, sanitation, education, law, peace and public order all depend upon improving those settlements. Since this question underlies many others, it demands a distinct effort.

Instead, ministers list four seed farms, ten breeding bulls in every taluk, ten poultry farms or ten houses built for ten Adi Dravidar families. Ruling-party members then calculate that ten houses were built, eight fell, and two remain, and ask how the remainder may be protected. Should we not be ashamed? When we ask that question, ruling-party members reply as though they too can ask us whether we feel shame. But responsibility rests with those in office. I want to know whether they have acted with concern and goodwill, examined who obstructs reform, and taken action against those impediments.

Many members have mentioned delays in allotting house sites for colonies. The report reveals the Government's attitude when it says, "It is sometimes complained that delay occurs in acquiring land for Harijans." The word "complained" does not acknowledge the fact. Members have said in this very House that acquisition remained incomplete after six years. If even they face that fate, I am dismayed to imagine what happens to ordinary people.

If law obstructs house-site allotment, pathways and burial grounds, why does the Minister not consult legal experts and act? This complaint was made not only this year but last year, the year before, and even when Sivashanmugam Pillai presided over this House. It has been discussed continuously for about ten years. Why have the difficulties not been removed? The report now finally refers to acting upon Law Commission recommendations to shorten the procedure, but the OCR of that sentence is incomplete. The delay itself is beyond dispute.

Money alone is insufficient; extensive public education is required. Two years ago, when a "Harijan Day" was proposed for propaganda, I repeatedly said in this House that the form was inadequate. It should become a common public day in which every party participates and through which cordial relations are created between the main village and the segregated settlement.

Some fear Opposition participation. Even if the Minister is unafraid, he wonders whether we might use the occasion to speak about national separation. We do not lack time, opportunity or places to discuss that subject; we need not attend Harijan Day for it. There is no need for fear or suspicion.

Make it possible for settlement residents to enter the village and villagers to visit settlement homes; for children from both places to play hand in hand; for elders to speak affectionately; and, if possible, for everyone to sit together and share a *samabandhi bhojanam*, an inter-caste common meal. If the day were conducted in that fashion, the status of settlement residents would certainly rise and real benefit would result.

I ask Minister Kakkan, when campaigning upon this question, especially among school and college students, to advise them: "During your holidays, Adi Dravidar students should stay as guests in caste-Hindu homes, and caste-Hindu students in Adi Dravidar homes." Whether or not such advice seems conventional for a minister, anyone truly concerned must insist upon action. Untouchability lies over society like dense fog.

No benefit comes from boasting, "All this happened only because our minister took charge," or "Only because Congress exists could so many MLAs enter this House." If that manner of argument continues, I too can say that only after our party took control of the Corporation could eight Adi Dravidars become Corporation members. Such pride produces no good.

We must act from justice, recognising the economic and social disparities between Adi Dravidars and other communities. One passage in the report made me laugh. It appears to say that a rural troupe travels from place to place performing *villuppattu* to abolish untouchability. Villuppattu itself is a declining form; the programme proposes using something already disappearing to make untouchability disappear.

The next item says the performers explain the Second Five-Year Plan. This suggests that each anti-untouchability occasion is being recruited into Government Plan publicity rather than that a dedicated campaign against untouchability is being conducted.

Minister Kakkan should not regard this merely as a budget debate. Whenever he has time, he should consult people from every party and establish methods to solve the problem. He should not view everything through party eyes, interpret criticism as a personal slight, become distressed, and then withdraw without seeking a remedy.

If funds continue to be allotted year after year at the present pace to improve settlements, lay roads, dig common wells, build schools and convert wasteland into cultivable land, when will the expected result arrive? Will it take twenty-five years, fifty years or a century? If you continue digging at this speed, the part already dug will fill again before the whole area is completed.

When the Cabinet asked for my views on the Third Five-Year Plan, I made a proposal that I repeat here. For five years, use the armed forces, their engineers, equipment and personnel to improve settlements, dig wells, build houses and roads, and convert wasteland into cultivable land. Only a plan committed to completing this reform within five years can succeed; annual allocations alone will never finish the work.

When you wage such a sacred campaign, our organisations may remain different, but those who favour progressive work will stand on one side and those who oppose it on the other. Kakkan may be certain that I will stand beside him, not beside orthodox defenders of caste. I will not worry whether he can speak fluent English. What matters is that he speaks good Tamil and carries out good work.

---NOTES---
The OCR heading prints the date as “74.02.1961”; contextual filename and date form support 14 February 1961. The source remains unchanged.

Historical community labels, including “Adi Dravidar” and “Harijan,” are retained where they identify period institutions, quoted programme names or the source's own terminology. The translation otherwise uses Scheduled Castes where the administrative category is meant.

Allocation figures fluctuate across the OCR between approximately Rs 2.55 crore, Rs 3.25 crore and a rounded Rs 3 crore. They are translated only where the local wording is clear and should be verified against the scans before citation.

“Physical target” is restored from the Tamil transliteration “பிஸிக்கல் டார்ஜட்” and means measurable work completed rather than nominal expenditure.

The name of the rural performance group on image 11 is illegible. Its use of *villuppattu* and simultaneous promotion of the Second Five-Year Plan are recoverable from context.
